 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the labor law, in relation to increasing the maximum time\nperiod for determining back pay and other liabilities for certain employees\nwho experience employment loss",
    "basePrintNo": "S8692",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8692-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 38,
       "fullName": "David Carlucci",
       "imgName": "370_david_carlucci.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 370,
       "sessionMemberId": 1191,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "CARLUCCI"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §860-g, Lab L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8692\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the labor law, in relation to increasing the maximum\ntime period for determining back pay and other liabilities for certain\nemployees who experience employment loss\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nIncreases the maximum amount of back pay receivable by employees when\nthere is a WARN Act violation from 60 days to 90 days to match the\n90-day notice requirement.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 removes the sixty-day cap on the number of days of back pay\nfor which an employer is liable when there is a WARN Act violation.\n\nSection 2 sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe WARN Act requires certain employers to provide at least 90 days'\nadvance notice to employees when there is a plant closing, relocation,\nor mass layoff. When a covered employer fails to provide timely notice,\nit is liable to its employees for back pay for the number of days for\nwhich there was a violation. However, this liability is capped at 60\ndays of back pay. As a result, workers receive the same back pay whether\nthe notice is 60 days late or 90 days late. This causes a perverse situ-\nation in which an employer has no incentive to give additional notice if\nit plans a plant closing, relocation, or mass layoff within thirty days.\nThis means that workers can suffer up to 30 days of additional lost pay.\n\nOf more practical importance in many situations is the fact that employ-\nees are suddenly without a paycheck, and therefore without a means to\nmake rent, buy food, and pay their medical bills. In this context, while\nan additional 30 days of violation means nothing financially to the\nemployer under current law, those 30 days are critical to a worker who\nneeds to immediately find a new job, especially because this issue only\noccurs in circumstances in which notice is given less than 30 days from\nthe date on which the employee will be laid off. With such limited time\nto find new employment, every day counts.\n\nThis is not a theoretical situation. Employees of the Doral Arrowwood\nResort in Rye Brook were told on Christmas Eve of 2019 that the hotel\nwould close on January 12, 2020, a notice period of just 19 days. Not\nonly were they very suddenly out of a job, because of the current 60-day\nlimitation on back pay, those 268 employees will not be compensated for\nthe eleven additional days for which notice was deficient under the WARN\nAct.\n\nThis bill extends the number of days of back pay for which an employer\nis liable to the full 90-day notice period. This change will eliminate\nthe current mismatch of incentives by linking the employer's financial\nliability to the full period of violation, thereby encouraging employers\nto give as much notice as possible in a time when it is arguably most\ncrucial to their workers.\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
